                <pb facs="1077" n="981" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg981"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">memorandum.</hi>
                </head>
                <p rendition="#center">[Substance of a telegram from Her Majesty’s consul-general,
                    Sir A. Gollan.]</p>
                <p>Her Majesty’s consul at Santiago telegraphs to me as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>I have seen the American prisoners in barrack in close proximity to the
                            town. They are supplied with as good food as the general scarcity
                            permits of. Lieutenant Hobson expresses satisfaction at everything; he
                            is well lodged. The lodging provided for the seamen is not so good; the
                            ventilation is poor, and they are not given sufficient facilities for
                            general exercise. I hope that there will be made an improvement in
                            regard to this last particular.</p>
                        <p>“In case of an attack by land, it is quite possible that the prisoners
                            would be exposed, as is the case with everybody else.</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
